+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Banding/Aliasing:** Naive application of standard polyhedral shading on the six discrete face orientations results in visible banding artifacts [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Binary Decision Artifacts:** The method discards sub-voxel information by making a binary decision (surface exists or not), leading to a hollow polygonal shell that may not accurately represent the true surface geometry [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Rendering every face of every voxel can be computationally expensive for dense volumes. Octree storage helps with sparse data [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Slab Extrusion (Staircase Representation)
-A variant where 2D slices are thresholded and extruded in the axial direction by the slice-to-slice distance. The resulting slabs are stacked to form a staircase-like representation [S1, unofficial]. It is simpler than the full 3D cuberille but suffers from similar discretization artifacts.
+A variant where slice data is thresholded and extruded in the axial direction by the slice-to-slice distance. These slabs are stacked and viewed using simple geometric routines to produce a staircase-like representation [S1, unofficial]. It is one of the first schemes used to view reconstructed contours [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, assuming constant-time face culling. With an octree, complexity depends on the sparsity and tree depth [S2, unofficial].
--   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the binary array. Octree storage is $O(S)$ where $S$ is the number of surface voxels, significantly less for sparse data [S2, unofficial].
--   **Performance**: Fast for dense, binary data. Octree traversal adds overhead but speeds up rendering of sparse volumes [S2, unofficial].
--   **Strengths**: Simple implementation; exact alignment with grid; efficient culling of internal faces [S1, unofficial].
--   **Limitations**: Aliasing/banding artifacts; loss of sub-voxel detail; difficult shading [S1, unofficial].
--   **When to use**: Binary segmented data where speed is critical and smoothness is not required.
--   **When not to use**: When smooth surfaces or partial volume information is needed.
+**Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, assuming constant-time face generation and culling. With an octree, traversal is proportional to the number of occupied leaf nodes [S2, unofficial].
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the binary array. $O(M)$ for the output mesh, where $M$ is the number of visible faces ($M \le 6N$). Octree storage reduces memory for sparse data [S2, unofficial].
+**Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+**Scaling:** Scales linearly with voxel count. Octree encoding increases traversal speed for sparse data but adds overhead for tree construction and traversal on conventional computers [S2, unofficial].
+**Strengths:** Simple to implement; preserves exact voxel grid alignment; fast for sparse data with octree optimization [S2, unofficial].
+**Limitations:** Blocky appearance (aliasing); shading difficulties due to discrete normals; loss of sub-voxel detail [S1, unofficial].
+**When to use:** When exact voxel alignment is required or for sparse volumetric data where octree acceleration is beneficial [S2, unofficial].
+**When not:** When smooth surface approximation is needed or high-fidelity shading is required without post-processing [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The cuberille model was first defined by Herman et al. [S1, unofficial]. Herman and Liu (1979) proposed the method of thresholding data into a binary array and rendering it as opaque cubes [S2, unofficial]. Meagher (1984) later proposed storing the binary array as an octree to improve traversal speed for sparse data [S2, unofficial]. The method is widely used in medical volume rendering for segmenting organs and structures [S1, unofficial].
+The Cuberille Model was defined first by Herman et al. [38] as the subset of voxels in a data volume that belong to an object or organ [S1, unofficial]. Herman and Liu [Herman 79] proposed the specific rendering method using a binary classifier and z-buffer or painter's algorithm [S2, unofficial]. Meagher [Meagher 84] proposed storing the binary array as an octree to accelerate traversal for sparse data [S2, unofficial]. The method is commonly used in medical imaging for visualizing segmented structures, such as in stereotactic neurosurgery planning [S1, unofficial].
 
